course: dictionary
lesson: 9565
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning is used. The word strain is a good example. Read the sentence first, then choose the meaning that fits best.
explain: «Shouting for a long time strains the vocal cords» means the cords are put under too much pressure. Strain can also mean to try very hard, as in «strain to hear» a faint sound. Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: «Doing extra does not speed healing and can strain the healing arm.» Here strain means to hurt by using too much. Context clues tell you the meaning, so you do not need to guess.
fact: Strain has more than one meaning, so always check the context.
fact: To strain means to try very hard, as in «strain to hear» faint sounds.
fact: To strain a muscle means to hurt it by stretching or using it too much; warming up helps stop muscle strains.
fact: To strain food or liquid means to pour it through a sieve to separate the solid bits, like pasta from water.
fact: Strain can also mean worry or pressure that makes someone tired or tense, and a strain is a type of plant, animal or germ, like a strain of flu.
exercise: «Shouting for a long time strains the vocal cords.» What does strains mean here? | Covers them with a soft cloth | Makes them sing better | Warms them up in the sun | *Puts too much pressure on them, so they can get hurt
exercise: A doctor says, «It is a new strain of flu.» What is a strain here? | A kind of muscle | A worry that makes you tired | A sieve for pasta | *A type of germ
exercise: «Lily had to strain her eyes to read the tiny print.» What does strain mean? | Pour them out | Close them tightly | Rub them gently | *Try very hard
test: You cook pasta, then strain it. What does strain mean here? | Stretch the pasta until it breaks | Heat it up again in the pan | Try very hard to lift the pot | *Pour it through a sieve to separate the solid bits from the water
test: Mum says, «You look under strain this week.» What does under strain mean? | Doing lots of weightlifting at the gym | Lying down under a blanket | Singing very loudly | *Under worry and pressure that makes you tired
exam: Which sentence uses strain to mean try very hard? | Pour the peas and strain them now | *She had to strain to hear the faint whisper | Mum caught a new strain of flu | The bridge can strain under heavy loads
